<b>Interview 3: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, company employee, female, born in 1980s, Hunan, presently wandering alone in Shanghai</b>
	
<p><b>Please talk about your impression of Germany or Kassel.</b>
<br>I already had a thorough understanding of Germany before this trip, because Grimm's Fairy Tales has accompanied me while I grew up, and since junior high school I began to like German football, literature and music. Although I didn’t major in German at college, I often read books about Germany, listened German music and watched German films. Based on those books and films I know Germany is a highly developed and civilized society, while I am also informed by my friends who study in Germany that Germany is not perfect. With high tax level and high rate of unemployment, although it’s an economical powerhouse in Europe, life in Germany is not as happy as life in Finland, Norway and Sweden. Before I went to Germany I was afraid that it might not be as good as I expected. However, when I finally arrived and got in touch with the simple and honest people in Kassel, all my doubts disappeared. When we walked on the streets in Kassel, people always greeted us and asked us about China or why we were here. When we were lost and didn’t know where the next exhibition hall was, there were always some warmhearted Germans to help us out. We also run into a professor and some students from the Chinese Department at University of Heidelberg. They were passionate and friendly.


<p>My deepest impression of this exhibition is that there are so many Chinese elements, such as the Chairs in the halls, the works by Mr. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and the 1001 Chinese people arriving here in turn. Maybe it’s not surprising to see Chinese people or culture in big cities like Frankfurt or Berlin, but in a small town like Kassel, local people must have seen for the first time so many Chinese people and Chinese things. It’s quite amazing for them. I think <i>Fairytale<i> by Mr. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; is extremely significant! This is because western media always reports China with prejudice. Some western people who have never been to China even believe Chinese still have braids, or China is still the way like 60 years ago. It’s a great opportunity to publicize China when 1001 Chinese people from different domains, careers and classes come to Kassel to join this exhibition.

<p><b>According to you, what should China learn from Germany?</b>
<br>Presently China is still developing and there are many shortcomings. Given a good opportunity there is wonderful prospect ahead, but in this rapidly changing society we have lost too many precious things. We forgot the most important thing: sincerity, brotherliness and kindness.
<p>What impressed me most about this trip of <i>Fairytale<i> is it makes me believe that there are still fairytales in this world. During the transient five days in Germany, I have met too many good hearted Germans and they helped me a lot. Some of their deeds are impossible in China. Maybe this is why Germany can rise up again in such a short time.
<p>I also learn from this experience that when you are in a totally different environment, you need to change yourself and get used to the local custom. If you often run red lights or throw litter around in China, you’d better not to bring these habits to Germany. It’s very bad because you not only embarrass yourself, but your nation too. Chinese should pay attention to their manner when they are abroad. It’s a good thing that this time we did very well.


<p><b>What’s the difference and similarity between Chinese people and western people?</b>
<br>What’s common between Chinese and western people is that we all pursue a life of high quality, but Chinese pay more attention to material quality than art and culture, while western people aspire more after a good spiritual life. They are highly religious and devout. Each city no matter big or small has several churches and book shops are everywhere.
<p>People here appreciate life better. Shops close before 9:00 pm or even 8:00 pm, supermarkets close before 10:00 pm. At weekends shops and supermarkets close all together. I know western people especially Europeans love to take short trip around or abroad with family at weekends. This represents their appreciation of life. Chinese people, on the other hand, go to department stores and supermarkets every weekend and this is really a tiresome lifestyle.


<p><b>Do you have religious belief?</b>
<br>I don’t have any now. Some of my friends believe in Protestantism, Catholicism or Buddhism. I think I am still too young and too immature to have any religious belief. After I get older I will choose a religion that fits me.

<p><b>What kind of problems are you normally concerned about? Are you concerned about international issues?</b>
<br>I am concerned about my everyday life, how to make everyday more meaningful, or is there any chance for me to run into something that would change me or my life. I feel anxious when I encounter frustrations or difficulties, but it doesn’t happen that often.
<p>International issues and energy issues are challenges that all the contemporary human kind has to face with. I feel no anxiety about extant international and energy issues because I believe we will solve these problems sooner or later with the common efforts from humanity. I hate wars and hope some day peace will be achieved in this world. But now Iraq, Afghanistan and some African countries are still in turmoil.

<p><b>What’s your opinion about environmental issues?</b>
<br>I don’t drive or smoke. I save water and electricity and I distinguish recycled and non-recycled rubbish. But this is merely tapping into the water. I think China has not done enough to protect environment, environmentally-friendly activities and measures are not popular enough. China has the largest population in the world, if half of the Chinese start to take actions, we will contribute most for the global environment.
<p>The biggest problem about China’s environmental protection is the lack of awareness. Another problem is how to balance economic development and environmental protection. I have a sense of crisis because China produces the second largest amount of greenhouse gas, just behind America. Dust storms, acid rain and poisonous gas are threatening our lives. Badly designed water-power engineering projects, illegal lumbering and mining and uncontrolled car exhaust, all of these are against environmental protection. I have seen a lot of wind driven generators in Germany, and China should borrow this idea to protect environment. The deterioration of environment makes me fear. I think it’s not only the problem of China, but of the whole world too.


<p><b>What’s wrong about China’s education?</b>
<br>As a direct victim of the college entrance system of China, I think China’s education system is too bad. The problem is the huge gap between education and reality, most of the things that we learn in schools are useless after we graduate. With so large a population, there are only a few Chinese students can enter colleges, but what’s strange is that those who work so hard to go to college still can’t find a decent job after graduation. I think higher education is not the only way out. China overestimates research education and ignores professional education. On the contrary, Germany’s education system is clearly framed, and students can choose schools that are suitable for their development and find jobs more easily after graduation.
<p>Higher education in the west is more humanistic. In Germany, for example, there is no uniform entrance exam and students are enrolled according to their grades and interests in high school. German universities are famous for “easy entrance, hard exit”. Of course for Chinese students the entrance requirements of German universities are quite strict. Germany doesn’t admit China’s high school degree. Chinese students have to hold offers from Chinese universities before they are qualified to apply for German universities. This clearly reflects the awkward position of China’s education. 
<p>German universities advocate freedom. There is no maximum registration period and students receive all kinds of benefits such as cheap transportation. But this also gives some people chances to take advantage by spending seven or eight years in college and keeping enjoying the benefits. Recently German universities start to arrange courses in line with international norms and introduce international system of degrees and periods such as 4 years for BA and 2 years for MA. They also start to charge token payment for higher education. Although this leads to protestation from most people, it does reduce deliberate prolonging of academic period and abusing student benefits at a large extent.

<p><b>Does the dialogue between China and the West urgent?</b>
<br>West has long been the pillar of global economy while China is the fastest developing country in recent decades. The dialogue between China and the West is necessary and urgent because we need to walk up to the world. Nowadays China has established a stable position in the world and many old powerful nations have been worrying about her rise up. Maybe from their position it’s not unreasonable to worry, but for me it’s unnecessary because China’s culture and history have determined that she will never become a nation like Japan.
<p>China has to reconcile her power and interests with other nations in her rise and the process will not be plain sailing. Presently China is still in a passive position in political, intellectual and security matters. I can see China’s great future but now she is not really strong enough. What’s most urgent for us is to reform educational and economic system and fight against corruption.


<p><b>What is the position of art in your heart?</b>
<br>Although I am not a professional artist, my life is never without art. Everyday, I listen to the music I like and read the books I like. Once a week I watch several classical or new movies. But I don’t have many chances to attend exhibitions. Last September the Biennale in Shanghai impressed me deeply, but in China such opportunity is rare.
<p>Another reason for me to like Germany is her symphony. Ever since I was a child my parents used to play famous symphonies for me with a recorder, most of which are masterpieces by Beethoven, Bach and Wagner. It’s a pity that my family is not rich enough for me to learn piano. I like <i>The Sorrows of Young Werther</i> and <i>Faust</i> by Goethe, poems by Heine and Grimm's Fairy Tales, Symphony No. 9 by Beethoven and Concerto for Violin in A Minor by Bach.


<p><b>Do you feel satisfied of your childhood?</b>
<br>Yes I do. My family is not rich at all but the sweet home environment provided me with a very good living environment. My parents never force me to learn anything I don’t like and I feel so luck that I never suffer from weekend schools. I remember those weekends when I went out with my friends to skate or to climb mountains, watch movies or watch boys play football.
<p>I get along very well with my parents. My father was quite tough on me and used to scold and even beat me. My mother was unhealthy and weak, I used to talk back and make her angry. I didn’t realize how childish I was until I grew up and started to understand her. My parents love me so much that I relied on them badly before I began to live alone. And it’s after I had to make a living on my own in Shanghai and Shenzhen and experienced many huge frustrations and failures that I finally grew up and really understood my parents. Now I love them even more than myself.


<p><b>What do you think of money?</b>
<br>My parents had suffered a lot and they had been unemployed for many years. I always wish to give them the best life. Everything I own I buy one for them too. I dislike those who live and eat in their parents’ even after they reach adult age. Since I went to college I bought my computer and everything I need on my own and I earned everything I own now. I am quite proud of myself. Although I am not rich and not able to better support my parents, I believe it’s only temporary. 
<p>Money can’t do everything but without money we can’t do anything. Money is very important for me because if I had money I could have gone to Germany in my second year in college, if we had money my mother would not have to suffer from disease, if we had money my father would not have to run a business at a loss after he lost his job, if I have money now I would not have to live in an unpleasant room at a remote town near Shanghai. I need money to improve my life and my parents’ lives, but I will never make money in illegal ways. I can do more than one jobs at the same time and work really hard to earn money.

<p><b>Do you like the company you work for now?</b>
<br>No I don’t like it at all. For three years I didn’t learn anything but spent my energy away. There’s too much injustice that makes me fearful and disappointed of society, and makes me miss the innocent college years. The biggest problem with my job is that I can’t exert my ability. 

<p><b>You said you could have gone to Germany?</b>
<br>Let me speak about my three experiences with Germany. I wanted to fulfill my dream by studying aboard because I didn’t enter a good college. In the second year I took the TestDaF test secretly. The results weren’t good, but I still got an offer from a German university. However, I had to give up because of some family issues.
<p>The second time I missed Germany is three years ago. I took part in a game show co-held by a famous Chinese football magazine and Bremen Club. I gave all the right answers and won a free trip to Germany to watch football games and visit Bremen Club. Unfortunately due to some financial discord between the two sponsors the plan was aborted.
<p>The third time is this <i>Fairytale</i> trip. I applied in February 29th but didn’t receive notification until 10:00 pm March 20th. I was called that night and a lady asked me if I had decided to give up. It turned out that I was chosen but didn’t receive the email from the organizing committee because I changed my email address. So, I want to thank Mr. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;, and the committee, and the <i>Fairytale</i>!


<p><b>Which fairytale moves you most?</b>
<br>It must be Cinderella. I feel that I am just like Cinderella, unremarkable and pursuing my dream quietly. And I also wish to meet my Prince Charming some day and put on the crystal shoes he gives me. It’s so dreamy!

